#: ../../source/docs/common-downloading-and-analyzing-data-logs-in-mission-planner.rst:5
#: 3dbda1e8346d40dd8151fe882f5fc9d3
msgid "Downloading and Analyzing Data Logs in Mission Planner"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-downloading-and-analyzing-data-logs-in-mission-planner.rst:7
#: 2d6b688748034bdd8ccd679b6276cf1e
msgid "Dataflash logs are stored on the autopilot and can be downloaded after a flight. By default, they are created after you first arm the vehicle. This topic explains how to configure and access Dataflash logs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-downloading-and-analyzing-data-logs-in-mission-planner.rst:12
#: 91592fb47b9b4818a0c2073e369e46e0
msgid "Depending on the autopilot type and configuration, the dataflash logs may be saved on a SD card, dataflash chip or streamed over MAVLink telemetry ports. The MAVLink option does require a high-speed telemetry port, typically 921600 baud."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-downloading-and-analyzing-data-logs-in-mission-planner.rst:14
#: e0cef60ebf9141e4810e05f0cd881f28
msgid "In addition to using Mission Planner, many other tools are available to users for log analysis: :ref:`MAVExplorer(part of MAVProxy installation) <using-mavexplorer-for-log-analysis>` and :ref:`Web-based tools <common-webtools>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-downloading-and-analyzing-data-logs-in-mission-planner.rst:18
#: 5e690abcc42e4bc99301d2b96ce38cf3
msgid ":ref:`Telemetry logs <planner:mission-planner-telemetry-logs>` (also known as \"tlogs\") collect similar information to dataflash logs (see :ref:`Diagnosing problems using Logs <common-diagnosing-problems-using-logs>` for more information)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-downloading-and-analyzing-data-logs-in-mission-planner.rst:20
#: 616d784cef354b37afef7940944b2f6b
msgid "If your vehicle is having trouble producing dataflash logs - including the infamous \"No IO heartbeat\" diagnostic message - try a different SD card.  You may also choose to test the card using a dedicated tool, such as ``H2testw``.  Low board voltages are also known to cause logging issues."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-downloading-and-analyzing-data-logs-in-mission-planner.rst:23
#: 9c129133a7d54ee9981a0926a2d97e4c
msgid "Logging Parameters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-downloading-and-analyzing-data-logs-in-mission-planner.rst:25
#: 3a62d19084394b7c984af5347022b5a4
msgid "Some commonly used parameters are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-downloading-and-analyzing-data-logs-in-mission-planner.rst:27
#: 947a6a0db5d74f638ba052e2723b7af7
msgid ":ref:`LOG_BACKEND_TYPE<LOG_BACKEND_TYPE>`: Bitmask for where to save logs to. Common values are \"0\" to disable logging, \"1\" (bit 0 set) to log to SD card file, \"2\"(bit 1 set) to stream over MAVLink and \"4\"(bit 2 set) to log to board dataflash memory, if equipped."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-downloading-and-analyzing-data-logs-in-mission-planner.rst:28
#: e8b3e975d5194fd2a6b440f517f64fbf
msgid ":ref:`LOG_BITMASK<LOG_BITMASK>`: Bitmask for what items are logged. Normally, use default value, or \"0\" to disable logging."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-downloading-and-analyzing-data-logs-in-mission-planner.rst:29
#: 918552e874ec4e43b505c1a2ac2aa0ab
msgid ":ref:`LOG_DISARMED<LOG_DISARMED>`: Setting to 1 will start logging when power is applied, rather than at the first arming of the vehicle. Useful when debugging pre-arm failures. Setting to 2 will only log on power application other than USB power to prevent logging while setting up on the bench. Setting to 3 will also erase any log in which the vehicle does not proceed to the armed stated. This prevents accumulating numerous logs while configuring on the bench or at the field. See :ref:`LOG_DARM_RATEMAX<LOG_DARM_RATEMAX>` also for managing log file sizes while logging disarmed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-downloading-and-analyzing-data-logs-in-mission-planner.rst:30
#: d1cbf45d4003440387def38b2ae1f3f9
msgid ":ref:`LOG_FILE_DSRMROT<LOG_FILE_DSRMROT>`: Setting this bit will force the creation of a new log file after disarming, waiting 15 seconds, and then re-arming. Normally, a log will be one file for every power cycle of the autopilot, beginning upon first arm."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-downloading-and-analyzing-data-logs-in-mission-planner.rst:31
#: 171393f40b5f4d38b3490728cad8e224
msgid ":ref:`LOG_FILE_MB_FREE<LOG_FILE_MB_FREE>`: This parameter sets the minimum free space on the logging media before logging begins. If this is not available, then older logs will be deleted to provide it during initialization. Default is 500MB."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-downloading-and-analyzing-data-logs-in-mission-planner.rst:32
#: c07ae48a8bae4ff8b7f20ee68aff2ccd
msgid ":ref:`LOG_FILE_RATEMAX<LOG_FILE_RATEMAX>`: This sets the maximum rate that streaming log messages will be logged to the file backend to limit file sizes. A value of zero(default) means no limit is applied to normal logging, which depends on the :ref:`SCHED_LOOP_RATE<SCHED_LOOP_RATE>` value ( 50Hz: Plane, 300Hz: QuadPlane/Rover, 400Hz: Copter, normally). Note that similarly, :ref:`LOG_BLK_RATEMAX<LOG_BLK_RATEMAX>` and :ref:`LOG_MAV_RATEMAX<LOG_MAV_RATEMAX>` perform the same optional limiting for the BLOCK logging and MAVLink logging streams, respectively."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-downloading-and-analyzing-data-logs-in-mission-planner.rst:33
#: daf59d3c9cc842338bd9cd35c2e543b8
msgid ":ref:`LOG_MAX_FILES<LOG_MAX_FILES>`: The maximum number of log file that will be written on dataflash or SD card before starting to rotate log number. Limit is a maximum of 500 logs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-downloading-and-analyzing-data-logs-in-mission-planner.rst:35
#: 8cb9082d1a2f4734bedae5da0280d4e7
msgid "If you suspect that you are missing logging entries due to excessive logging speed, you can check the DSF.Dp log message for the amount of missed entries."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-downloading-and-analyzing-data-logs-in-mission-planner.rst:37
#: 3924ae684c724a93b1cca76cd8ee7462
msgid "Logging of the continuously streaming log messages, such as attitude, sensors, etc. can be paused by using the ``RCx_OPTION`` auxiliary function \"164\" on a transmitter channel. Switching this channel high will pause these messages, but not events, mode changes, warnings, etc. This allows autopilots with limited logging capabilities (ie using Block logging to chip memory and no SD card) to log only when desired during the flight, as during tuning phases or determination of TECs parameters, etc. You can also eliminate unneeded log messages using :ref:`LOG_BITMASK<LOG_BITMASK>` to reduce log size"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-downloading-and-analyzing-data-logs-in-mission-planner.rst:40
#: ad737bcb0d614d739f74fe76ef891ff1
msgid "Replay Logging"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-downloading-and-analyzing-data-logs-in-mission-planner.rst:42
#: 92b5fdd18c934979b252903d061a1a35
msgid "ArduPilot has the ability to log in a fashion that solutions to EKF/AHRS issues can be more easily verified by actually re-playing a log against code changes to see if the solution results in the desired, corrected behavior. This requires that the logs showing the issue to be worked on be made with logging active during disarmed periods (with :ref:`LOG_DISARMED<LOG_DISARMED>` set to a non-zero value, preferably 3) and :ref:`LOG_REPLAY<LOG_REPLAY>` =1 , thereby logging more sensor data than normal."
msgstr ""

